 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 3.08 — Gauss–Bonnet\n\nThis is the summit of Stage 3, and arguably of the whole journey so far. Everything since 1.04 has been circling it.\n\n**Local form.** For a region $R$ on a surface, bounded by a piecewise-smooth curve with exterior angles $\\theta_i$ at its corners:\n\n$$\\iint_R K\\,dA \\;+\\; \\oint_{\\partial R}\\kappa_g\\,ds \\;+\\; \\sum_i \\theta_i \\;=\\; 2\\pi .$$\n\n**Global form.** For a compact surface without boundary:\n\n$$\\iint_S K\\,dA \\;=\\; 2\\pi\\,\\chi(S),$$\n\nwhere $\\chi = 2 - 2g$ is the Euler characteristic, $g$ the number of handles.\n\nThe global form is the astonishing one. On the left is an integral of **curvature** — a quantity depending on the metric, changing under every dent and stretch. On the right is $\\chi$, a **topological** invariant that counts holes and knows nothing about geometry. Deform a sphere however you like: push in a dent, and $K$ goes negative there, but it must increase somewhere else by exactly the compensating amount. The total is stuck at $4\\pi$.\n\n**Three special cases already met, now unified:**\n\n| | $\\oint\\kappa_g$ | $\\iint K$ | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| geodesic triangle, $K = -1$ | $0$ | $-\\text{Area}$ | $\\text{Area} = \\pi - \\sum\\alpha_i$ (2.06) |\n| geodesic triangle, $K = +1$ | $0$ | $+\\text{Area}$ | $\\text{Area} = \\sum\\alpha_i - \\pi$ (2.07) |\n| plane curve, $K = 0$ | $2\\pi$ | $0$ | the Umlaufsatz (3.01) |\n\nEach was derived separately and looked like a different fact. They are one theorem.\n\nThe pattern — **integrate a local quantity, get a topological invariant** — is the template for the deepest results in geometry. Chern–Weil (5.06) generalises it to characteristic classes; the Atiyah–Singer index theorem, signposted in 6.07, is its final form.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\npd(s,var)   := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), s)$\nts(e)       := trigsimp(e)$\nFFF(s,u_,v_) := block([su,sv], su : pd(s,u_), sv : pd(s,v_),\n  matrix([ts(su.su), ts(su.sv)], [ts(su.sv), ts(sv.sv)]))$\nunitnormal(s,u_,v_) := block([n], n : cross3(pd(s,u_), pd(s,v_)),\n  map(ts, n/sqrt(ts(n . n))))$\nSFF(s,u_,v_) := block([nn,suu,suv,svv],\n  nn : unitnormal(s,u_,v_),\n  suu : pd(pd(s,u_),u_), suv : pd(pd(s,u_),v_), svv : pd(pd(s,v_),v_),\n  matrix([ts(suu.nn), ts(suv.nn)], [ts(suv.nn), ts(svv.nn)]))$\nKof(s,u_,v_) := ts( determinant(SFF(s,u_,v_))/determinant(FFF(s,u_,v_)) )$\ndAof(s,u_,v_):= ts( sqrt(determinant(FFF(s,u_,v_))) )$\n\n/* Total curvature ∫∫ K dA over a closed surface */\ntotalK(s, u_, v_, u0, u1, v0, v1) :=\n  integrate( integrate( ratsimp( Kof(s,u_,v_)*dAof(s,u_,v_) ), u_, u0, u1),\n             v_, v0, v1)$\n\nassume(R > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\n\n/* --- The sphere: χ = 2 --- */\nsphere : [R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*cos(u)]$\nprint(\"SPHERE (genus 0, χ = 2):\")$\nprint(\"   K    = \", Kof(sphere,u,v))$\nprint(\"   dA   = \", dAof(sphere,u,v))$\nprint(\"   K·dA = \", ratsimp(Kof(sphere,u,v)*dAof(sphere,u,v)),\n      \"   — the radius cancels entirely\")$\ntsph : totalK(sphere, u, v, 0, %pi, 0, 2*%pi)$\nprint(\"   ∫∫ K dA = \", tsph, \"   and 2πχ = \", 2*%pi*2, \" = \", float(4*%pi))$\nprint(\"   independent of R: a big sphere has small K over a large area ✓\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "SPHERE (genus 0, χ = 2):\n   K    =  1/R^2\n   dA   =  R^2*sin(u)\n   K·dA =  sin(u)    — the radius cancels entirely\n   ∫∫ K dA =  4*%pi    and 2πχ =  4*%pi  =  12.566370614359172\n   independent of R: a big sphere has small K over a large area ✓"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The torus: χ = 0 --- */\ntorus : [(3 + cos(u))*cos(v), (3 + cos(u))*sin(v), sin(u)]$\nKt : Kof(torus,u,v)$  dAt : dAof(torus,u,v)$\nprint(\"TORUS (genus 1, χ = 0):\")$\nprint(\"   K    = \", Kt, \"   — changes sign with cos u\")$\nprint(\"   K·dA = \", ratsimp(Kt*dAt))$\nttor : integrate( integrate( ratsimp(Kt*dAt), u, 0, 2*%pi), v, 0, 2*%pi)$\nprint(\"   ∫∫ K dA = \", ttor, \"   and 2πχ = 0 ✓\")$\nprint(\"   the positive outer half exactly cancels the negative inner half\")$\n\n/* Split the integral to see the cancellation */\nouter : integrate( integrate( ratsimp(Kt*dAt), u, -%pi/2, %pi/2), v, 0, 2*%pi)$\ninner : integrate( integrate( ratsimp(Kt*dAt), u, %pi/2, 3*%pi/2), v, 0, 2*%pi)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   outer half (K > 0): \", outer, \" = \", float(outer))$\nprint(\"   inner half (K < 0): \", inner, \" = \", float(inner))$\nprint(\"   sum               : \", ratsimp(outer + inner))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "TORUS (genus 1, χ = 0):\n   K    =  cos(u)/(cos(u)+3)    — changes sign with cos u\n   K·dA =  (cos(u)*sqrt(cos(u)^2+6*cos(u)+9))/(cos(u)+3)\n   ∫∫ K dA =  0    and 2πχ = 0 ✓\n   the positive outer half exactly cancels the negative inner half\n   outer half (K > 0):  4*%pi  =  12.566370614359172\n   inner half (K < 0):  -(4*%pi)  =  -12.566370614359172\n   sum               :  0"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- A lumpy ellipsoid: K varies enormously, the total cannot move --- */\naa : 1$  bb : 3/2$  cc : 5/2$\nellipsoid : [aa*sin(u)*cos(v), bb*sin(u)*sin(v), cc*cos(u)]$\nKe  : Kof(ellipsoid, u, v)$\ndAe : dAof(ellipsoid, u, v)$\n\nprint(\"ELLIPSOID with semi-axes \", [aa, bb, cc], \" (still genus 0, χ = 2):\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   K varies hugely over the surface:\")$\nprint(\"      point                     K\")$\nfor pt in [[%pi/2, 0], [%pi/2, %pi/2], [%pi/100, 0], [%pi/4, %pi/4]] do\n  print(\"      u=\", float(pt[1]), \" v=\", float(pt[2]), \"   \",\n        float(subst([u=pt[1], v=pt[2]], Ke)))$\nprint(\"   (near the poles, where the ellipsoid is most sharply curved,\")$\nprint(\"    K is large; around the equator it is far smaller)\")$\n\n/* Integrate K dA numerically over the whole surface, by a midpoint rule. */\nintegrand : ratsimp( Ke * dAe )$\nKfun(uu, vv) := float( subst([u = uu, v = vv], integrand) )$\n\ntotal_num(nu, nv) := block([hu, hv, s],\n  hu : float(%pi/nu),  hv : float(2*%pi/nv),  s : 0.0,\n  for i : 0 thru nu-1 do\n    for j : 0 thru nv-1 do\n      s : s + Kfun( (i+0.5)*hu, (j+0.5)*hv ),\n  s*hu*hv )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   grid        ∫∫ K dA (midpoint rule)\")$\nfor n in [20, 40, 80] do\n  print(\"   \", n, \"×\", 2*n, \"      \", total_num(n, 2*n))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   4π = \", float(4*%pi), \"   — converging to 2πχ = 4π ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The ellipsoid is a badly dented sphere. Its curvature is nowhere near\")$\nprint(\"constant, yet the integral is pinned to the same 4π. Deforming the\")$\nprint(\"surface moves curvature around; it cannot create or destroy any.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "ELLIPSOID with semi-axes  [1,3/2,5/2]  (still genus 0, χ = 2):\n   K varies hugely over the surface:\n      point                     K\n      u= 1.5707963267948966  v= 0.0     0.07111111111111111\n      u= 1.5707963267948966  v= 1.5707963267948966     0.36\n      u= 0.031415926535897934  v= 0.0     2.7492229557548757\n      u= 0.7853981633974483  v= 0.7853981633974483     0.3654613632470227\n   (near the poles, where the ellipsoid is most sharply curved,\n    K is large; around the equator it is far smaller)\n   grid        ∫∫ K dA (midpoint rule)\n    20 × 40        12.621568683309844\n    40 × 80        12.579908594905682\n    80 × 160        12.569739969029541\n   4π =  12.566370614359172    — converging to 2πχ = 4π ✓\nThe ellipsoid is a badly dented sphere. Its curvature is nowhere near\nconstant, yet the integral is pinned to the same 4π. Deforming the\nsurface moves curvature around; it cannot create or destroy any."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The local form on a geodesic triangle --- */\n/* For a triangle bounded by geodesics, κ_g ≡ 0, so Gauss–Bonnet reads\n      ∫∫ K dA + Σ (exterior angles) = 2π,\n   and with exterior θᵢ = π − αᵢ this becomes  ∫∫K dA = Σαᵢ − π.  */\n\nprint(\"geodesic triangle on the UNIT SPHERE (K = 1): the octant\")$\nprint(\"   interior angles αᵢ = π/2, π/2, π/2\")$\nalphas : [%pi/2, %pi/2, %pi/2]$\nthetas : map(lambda([a], %pi - a), alphas)$\narea_oct : %pi/2$          /* one eighth of 4π */\nprint(\"   exterior angles θᵢ = \", thetas)$\nprint(\"   area              = \", area_oct, \" = \", float(area_oct))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   ∫∫K dA + ∮κ_g ds + Σθᵢ = \", area_oct, \" + 0 + \", apply(\"+\", thetas),\n      \" = \", ratsimp(area_oct + apply(\"+\", thetas)))$\nprint(\"   2π = \", 2*%pi, \"   ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   rearranged: ∫∫K dA = Σαᵢ − π  ⟹  Area = \", apply(\"+\",alphas), \" − π = \",\n      ratsimp(apply(\"+\",alphas) - %pi), \"   — Girard's theorem (2.07) ✓\")$\n\n/* The same identity with K = −1 gives the hyperbolic law of 2.06 */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"with K = −1 the same equation gives −Area = Σαᵢ − π,\")$\nprint(\"   i.e. Area = π − Σαᵢ — the hyperbolic area law of 2.06 ✓\")$\n\n/* And with K = 0 and a smooth closed curve, no corners: */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"with K = 0 and a smooth closed boundary (no corners, so Σθᵢ = 0):\")$\nprint(\"   ∮ κ_g ds = 2π — the Umlaufsatz of 3.01 ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Three results from three different notebooks, all the same theorem.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "geodesic triangle on the UNIT SPHERE (K = 1): the octant\n   interior angles αᵢ = π/2, π/2, π/2\n   exterior angles θᵢ =  [%pi/2,%pi/2,%pi/2]\n   area              =  %pi/2  =  1.5707963267948966\n   ∫∫K dA + ∮κ_g ds + Σθᵢ =  %pi/2  + 0 +  (3*%pi)/2  =  2*%pi\n   2π =  2*%pi    ✓\n   rearranged: ∫∫K dA = Σαᵢ − π  ⟹  Area =\n  (3*%pi)/2  − π =  %pi/2    — Girard's theorem (2.07) ✓\nwith K = −1 the same equation gives −Area = Σαᵢ − π,\n   i.e. Area = π − Σαᵢ — the hyperbolic area law of 2.06 ✓\nwith K = 0 and a smooth closed boundary (no corners, so Σθᵢ = 0):\n   ∮ κ_g ds = 2π — the Umlaufsatz of 3.01 ✓\nThree results from three different notebooks, all the same theorem."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Curvature across the ellipsoid, against the sphere it is a deformation of.\n   Both integrate to 4π; only the distribution differs. */\nuu_s : makelist(float(%pi*k/300), k, 1, 299)$\nKell(vv) := [discrete, uu_s,\n             map(lambda([t], float(subst([u=t, v=vv], Ke))), uu_s)]$\nKsph : [discrete, uu_s, makelist(1.0, k, 1, 299)]$\n\nprint(\"K along a meridian of the ellipsoid (semi-axes 1, 1.5, 2.5):\")$\nprint(\"   at the pole    u→0    : K ≈ \", float(subst([u=0.01,v=0], Ke)))$\nprint(\"   at the equator u=π/2  : K = \", float(subst([u=%pi/2,v=0], Ke)),\n      \" (v=0) and \", float(subst([u=%pi/2,v=%pi/2], Ke)), \" (v=π/2)\")$\nprint(\"   ratio max/min ≈ \", float(subst([u=0.01,v=0],Ke)/subst([u=%pi/2,v=0],Ke)))$\n\nplot2d([ Kell(0), Kell(%pi/2), Ksph ],\n       [x, 0, 3.15], [y, 0, 3],\n       [style, [lines,3],[lines,3],[lines,2]],\n       [legend, \"ellipsoid, v = 0\", \"ellipsoid, v = π/2\", \"unit sphere (K ≡ 1)\"],\n       [xlabel, \"u — colatitude\"], [ylabel, \"Gaussian curvature K\"],\n       [title, \"same total curvature 4π, wildly different distributions\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "K along a meridian of the ellipsoid (semi-axes 1, 1.5, 2.5):\n   at the pole    u→0    : K ≈  2.774863503447319\n   at the equator u=π/2  : K =  0.07111111111111111  (v=0) and  0.36  (v=π/2)\n   ratio max/min ≈  39.02151801722792"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/svg+xml": [
       "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"  standalone=\"no\"?>\n<svg \n width=\"600\" height=\"480\"\n viewBox=\"0 0 600 480\"\n xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"\n xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"\n>\n\n<title>Gnuplot</title>\n<desc>Produced by GNUPLOT 6.0 patchlevel 4 </desc>\n\n<g id=\"gnuplot_canvas\">\n\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"600\" height=\"480\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<defs>\n\n\t<circle id='gpDot' r='0.5' stroke-width='0.5' stroke='currentColor'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt0' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 h2 M0,-1 v2'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt1' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,-1 L1,1 M1,-1 L-1,1'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt2' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 L1,0 M0,-1 L0,1 M-1,-1 L1,1 M-1,1 L1,-1'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt3' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt4' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' fill='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<circle id='gpPt5' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' cx='0' cy='0' r='1'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt5' id='gpPt6' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt7' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,-1.33 L-1.33,0.67 L1.33,0.67 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt8' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt9' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(180)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt9' id='gpPt10' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt3' id='gpPt11' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(45)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt11' id='gpPt12' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt13' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,1.330 L1.265,0.411 L0.782,-1.067 L-0.782,-1.076 L-1.265,0.411 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt13' id='gpPt14' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<filter id='textbox' filterUnits='objectBoundingBox' x='0' y='0' height='1' width='1'>\n\t  <feFlood flood-color='whit