   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\iint_R K\\,dA + \\oint_{\\partial R}\\kappa_g\\,ds + \\sum_i\\theta_i = 2\\pi \\qquad\\qquad \\iint_S K\\,dA = 2\\pi\\chi(S)$$\n\n| Surface | $\\chi$ | $\\iint K\\,dA$ | $2\\pi\\chi$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| sphere (any radius) | $2$ | $4\\pi$ | $4\\pi$ ✓ |\n| ellipsoid $(1, 1.5, 2.5)$ | $2$ | $12.5697 \\to 4\\pi$ | $4\\pi$ ✓ |\n| torus | $0$ | $0$ (outer $+4\\pi$, inner $-4\\pi$) | $0$ ✓ |\n\nThree computations worth keeping:\n\n**The sphere's radius cancels.** $K\\,dA = (1/R^2)(R^2\\sin u)\\,du\\,dv = \\sin u\\,du\\,dv$. A large sphere has small curvature over a large area; the product is fixed. The result *cannot* depend on $R$, and the algebra shows why.\n\n**The torus cancels exactly.** The outer half contributes $+4\\pi$, the inner half $-4\\pi$. Not approximately — exactly, and independently of both radii.\n\n**The ellipsoid is the striking case.** Its curvature ranges over a factor of **39** between pole and equator, and varies with longitude as well since $a \\ne b$. The midpoint-rule integral converges $12.6216 \\to 12.5799 \\to 12.5697$ against $4\\pi = 12.5664$. Deforming a sphere redistributes curvature; it cannot change the total.\n\nAnd the local form recovers, from one equation, three results derived separately:\n\n| $K$ | Boundary | Recovers |\n|---|---|---|\n| $+1$ | geodesic triangle | Girard: Area $= \\sum\\alpha_i - \\pi$ (2.07) |\n| $-1$ | geodesic triangle | Area $= \\pi - \\sum\\alpha_i$ (2.06) |\n| $0$ | smooth closed curve | Umlaufsatz: $\\oint\\kappa_g = 2\\pi$ (3.01) |\n\n## Stage 3 in retrospect\n\nThe stage set out to replace Klein's homogeneous geometries with a local theory, and the arc of $K$ tells the story:\n\n1. **3.04** — defined $K$ *extrinsically*, from the second fundamental form and the unit normal.\n2. **3.06** — proved it *intrinsic*: computable from $E, F, G$, so no ambient space is needed.\n3. **3.08** — showed its integral is *topological*: independent of the metric entirely.\n\nEach step removes a dependency. By the end, a quantity introduced as \"how the surface bends in space\" turns out to know nothing about space, and in aggregate nothing about geometry either — only about holes.\n\nThat is the pattern the rest of the journey follows. Stage 5's Chern–Weil theory is the general machine for building topological invariants out of curvature, and Gauss–Bonnet is its first and simplest instance; the Gauss–Bonnet–Chern theorem extends this exact statement to even-dimensional manifolds.\n\n## Next\n\n**3.09 — Minimal Surfaces** closes Stage 3 with the other curvature, $H$. Where $K$ led to topology, $H = 0$ leads to a variational problem: soap films, the catenoid and helicoid met in 3.06, and Plateau's problem.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §4-5 gives both forms of Gauss–Bonnet with full proofs, including the triangulation argument for the global version.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapter 13, the most accessible treatment, with the local form proved carefully before the global one.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 9 proves Gauss–Bonnet and Chapter 10 develops the Riemannian analogues; the Gauss–Bonnet–Chern generalisation is discussed there.\n- ★ Naber, G.L. (1997) *Topology, Geometry, and Gauge Fields*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-2742-5. For the route from Gauss–Bonnet to characteristic classes, taken up in Stage 5.\n- Chern, S.-S. (1944) 'A simple intrinsic proof of the Gauss-Bonnet formula for closed Riemannian manifolds', *Annals of Mathematics*, 45(4), pp. 747–752.\n\n**On what is verified here.** The theorem is not proved in this notebook — the proof needs a triangulation argument and the Euler characteristic's combinatorial invariance, both of which belong to [topology-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey) Stage 2. What is verified is the *conclusion*, on three surfaces of two different genera, including one where the integral had to be computed numerically because no closed form was available.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
